IMPORT prelude

-- Legitimacy Act 1934 (Singapore) -- when a child born to unmarried parents becomes legitimate on their later marriage (s 3),
-- recognition of legitimation under a foreign father's law (s 9), declarations of legitimacy (s 4), the property and inheritance
-- rights of a legitimated person (ss 5, 7, 11), and the mutual intestacy rights of an illegitimate child and the mother (s 10).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation ("version in force from 5/12/2025"), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/LA1934.txt. The revised edition incorporates amendments up to 1 December 2021; the latest amendment
-- annotated is Act 19 of 2025 (wef 05/12/2025) against s 4(1). s 3(4) and the Schedule were deleted by Act 17 of 2021 (wef
-- 29/05/2022). The arrangement of sections at the top of the deposit is out of step with the body by one line; this encoding follows
-- the body's numbering (s 3 legitimation, s 4 declarations, s 5 property, ... s 11 saving).
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Chosen for its everyday-life relevance: it decides whether a child born before the parents married is legitimate,
-- and whether that child, or a child who stays illegitimate, can inherit from a parent who dies without a will. (An automated count
-- found 2 of the 527 deposited Singapore Acts citing it by title; that count undercounts, and is not a measure of importance.)
-- Not encoded: s 4(2), (3), (5), (7)-(9) (affidavits, costs, citation of parties, rules); s 6 (who succeeds to a legitimated person,
-- which defers to the general law of intestacy); s 8 (maintenance and statutory claims, which simply apply "as if he had been born
-- legitimate"); the general intestacy shares themselves (Intestate Succession Act); and what "domiciled" means.
--
-- Dates are written as numbers YYYYMMDD (e.g. 19340518).
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Legitimacy Act 1934 -- legitimation`

GIVEN `first date` IS A NUMBER, `second date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the later of` MEANS
    IF `first date` GREATER THAN `second date` THEN `first date` ELSE `second date`

§§ `3 -- legitimation by subsequent marriage of parents`

-- s 3(1): "where the parents of an illegitimate person marry or have married one another ... the marriage shall, if the father or
-- mother of the illegitimate person was or is at the date of the marriage domiciled in Singapore, render that person, if living,
-- legitimate from 18 May 1934 or from the date of the marriage, whichever last happens."
-- s 3(2): "Nothing in this Act shall operate to legitimate a person unless the marriage ... was solemnised and registered" under the
-- repealed Christian Marriage or Civil Marriage Ordinance "or unless that marriage was registered or deemed to be registered under
-- the Women's Charter 1961."
-- s 2: "date of legitimation" means the date of the marriage, "or, where the marriage occurred before 18 May 1934, that day".
GIVEN `the parents married each other` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the father was domiciled in Singapore at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the mother was domiciled in Singapore at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the marriage was registered as section 3(2) requires` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the child was living at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the child is legitimated under section 3` IF
        `the parents married each other`
    AND (`the father was domiciled in Singapore at the marriage` OR `the mother was domiciled in Singapore at the marriage`)
    AND `the marriage was registered as section 3(2) requires`
    AND `the child was living at the marriage`

GIVEN `the marriage date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the section 3 date of legitimation` MEANS
    `the later of` 19340518 `the marriage date`

§§ `9 -- legitimation by extraneous law`

-- s 9(1): where the parents marry "and the father of the illegitimate person was or is, at the time of the marriage, domiciled in a
-- country other than Singapore, by the law of which the illegitimate person became legitimated by virtue of the subsequent marriage,
-- that person, if living, shall within Singapore be recognised as having been so legitimated from 17 May 1934 or from the date of the
-- marriage, whichever last happens, notwithstanding that his father was not at the time of the birth of that person domiciled in a
-- country in which legitimation by subsequent marriage was permitted by law."
-- Only the FATHER's domicile counts here; s 3 accepts either parent's. The start date is 17 May 1934, one day earlier than s 3's.
-- Whether s 3(2)'s registration requirement ("Nothing in this Act shall operate to legitimate") also governs s 9 recognition is not
-- said; this encoding does NOT apply it to s 9 (an inference: s 9 recognises a foreign legitimation rather than effecting one).
GIVEN `the parents married each other` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the father was domiciled abroad at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the law of the father's domicile legitimated the child by the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the child was living at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the father's domicile at the birth permitted legitimation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the child is recognised as legitimated under section 9` IF
        `the parents married each other`
    AND `the father was domiciled abroad at the marriage`
    AND `the law of the father's domicile legitimated the child by the marriage`
    AND `the child was living at the marriage`

GIVEN `the marriage date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the section 9 date of legitimation` MEANS
    `the later of` 19340517 `the marriage date`

§§ `4 -- declarations of legitimacy`

-- s 4(1): "A person claiming that he or his parent or any remoter ancestor became or has become a legitimated person may, whether
-- domiciled in Singapore or elsewhere, and whether a citizen of Singapore or not, apply to the court by an originating application";
-- the decree "shall be binding to all intents and purposes on the Government and on all persons whomsoever."
-- s 4(4): a copy must be served on the Attorney-General.
-- s 4(6): the decree "shall not in any case prejudice any person -- (a) if it is subsequently proved to have been obtained by fraud
-- or collusion; or (b) unless that person has been cited or made a party to the proceedings or is the heir at law, next of kin, or
-- other real or personal representative of, or derives title under or through, a person so cited or made a party."
GIVEN `claims that he or an ancestor became legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the applicant is domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the applicant is a Singapore citizen` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `may apply for a declaration of legitimacy` IF
    `claims that he or an ancestor became legitimated`

GIVEN `the decree was proved to be obtained by fraud or collusion` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the person was cited or made a party` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the person is heir, next of kin, representative of, or claims through, such a party` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decree can prejudice this person` IF
        (NOT `the decree was proved to be obtained by fraud or collusion`)
    AND (`the person was cited or made a party` OR `the person is heir, next of kin, representative of, or claims through, such a party`)

§ `Legitimacy Act 1934 -- property and succession`

§§ `5, 11 -- rights of legitimated persons to take interests in property`

-- s 3(3): legitimation does not enable the person "or his spouse, children or remoter issue to take any interest in property save as
-- hereinafter expressly provided".
-- s 5(1): a legitimated person and his spouse, children or remoter issue may take "(a) in the estate of an intestate dying after the
-- date of legitimation; (b) under any disposition coming into operation after the date of legitimation, in the like manner as if the
-- legitimated person had been born legitimate."
-- s 5(3): "This section applies only if and so far as a contrary intention is not expressed in the disposition".
-- s 11: nothing affects a disposition coming into operation before 18 May 1934, or rights under the intestacy of a person dying
-- before that date. (With s 3 dates no earlier than 18 May 1934, s 5's "after" already excludes those cases.)
GIVEN `the date of legitimation` IS A NUMBER, `the intestate's date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `takes in the intestate's estate as if born legitimate` IF
        `the intestate's date of death` GREATER THAN `the date of legitimation`
    AND `the intestate's date of death` AT LEAST 19340518

GIVEN `the date of legitimation` IS A NUMBER,
      `the disposition's operative date` IS A NUMBER,
      `the disposition expresses a contrary intention` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `takes under the disposition as if born legitimate` IF
        `the disposition's operative date` GREATER THAN `the date of legitimation`
    AND `the disposition's operative date` AT LEAST 19340518
    AND (NOT `the disposition expresses a contrary intention`)

-- s 5(2): where property depends on "the relative seniority of the children of any person", a legitimated person ranks "as if he or
-- they had been born on the day when he or they became legitimated", and those legitimated at the same time rank among themselves "in
-- order of seniority".
GIVEN `birth date` IS A NUMBER, `was legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN, `legitimation date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the ranking date` MEANS
    IF `was legitimated` THEN `legitimation date` ELSE `birth date`

GIVEN `first birth date` IS A NUMBER, `first was legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN, `first legitimation date` IS A NUMBER,
      `second birth date` IS A NUMBER, `second was legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN, `second legitimation date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the first child ranks as senior` IF
        `the ranking date` `first birth date` `first was legitimated` `first legitimation date`
            LESS THAN `the ranking date` `second birth date` `second was legitimated` `second legitimation date`
    OR  (    `first was legitimated` AND `second was legitimated`
         AND `first legitimation date` EQUALS `second legitimation date`
         AND `first birth date` LESS THAN `second birth date`)

§§ `7 -- illegitimate person dying before the parents' marriage`

-- s 7: where an illegitimate person "dies on or after 18 May 1934 and before the marriage of his parents leaving any spouse, children
-- or remoter issue living at the date of the marriage, then if that person would, if living at the time of the marriage of his
-- parents, have become a legitimated person", the property provisions for the spouse and issue of a legitimated person apply "as if
-- that person had been a legitimated person and the date of the marriage of his parents had been the date of legitimation."
GIVEN `the person's date of death` IS A NUMBER, `the parents' marriage date` IS A NUMBER,
      `left a spouse or issue living at the marriage` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `would have been legitimated if living` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the spouse and issue are treated as those of a legitimated person` IF
        `the person's date of death` AT LEAST 19340518
    AND `the person's date of death` LESS THAN `the parents' marriage date`
    AND `left a spouse or issue living at the marriage`
    AND `would have been legitimated if living`

§§ `10 -- illegitimate child and mother succeeding on intestacy of the other`

-- s 10(1): where on or after 18 May 1934 "the mother of an illegitimate child, the child not being a legitimated person, dies intestate
-- ... and does not leave any legitimate issue her surviving, the illegitimate child, or if he is dead his issue, shall be entitled to
-- take any interest therein to which he or his issue would have been entitled if he had been born legitimate."
-- s 10(2): where on or after 18 May 1934 such a child dies intestate, "his mother, if surviving, shall be entitled to take any interest
-- therein to which she would have been entitled if the child had been born legitimate and she had been the only surviving parent."
-- The section gives no corresponding right to or from the FATHER of an illegitimate child.
GIVEN `the mother's date of death` IS A NUMBER, `the child was legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the mother died intestate` IS A BOOLEAN, `the mother left legitimate issue surviving` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the illegitimate child takes from the mother's intestacy under section 10` IF
        `the mother's date of death` AT LEAST 19340518
    AND (NOT `the child was legitimated`)
    AND `the mother died intestate`
    AND (NOT `the mother left legitimate issue surviving`)

GIVEN `the child's date of death` IS A NUMBER, `the child was legitimated` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the child died intestate` IS A BOOLEAN, `the mother survived` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the mother takes from the illegitimate child's intestacy under section 10` IF
        `the child's date of death` AT LEAST 19340518
    AND (NOT `the child was legitimated`)
    AND `the child died intestate`
    AND `the mother survived`
